# NIT Academy Infrastructure Job Readiness Training Manual

Instructor edition with student exercises and assessment answer keys  
Prepared for Nadeem Siddiqi  
Version 1.0 • 1 October 2026

## 1 Purpose and realistic outcomes

This manual trains candidates to build, operate, troubleshoot, secure, and recover a small Linux infrastructure environment. It uses your Dell PowerEdge R620, an XCP-ng hypervisor managed through Xen Orchestra, one Cisco router, and one Cisco switch. It connects each exercise to the supplied job requirements and produces evidence students can explain in an interview.

**One week is a useful assessment and revision sprint for students who already administer Linux. It is not enough to create the strong working experience described in this job specification.** No training program can guarantee that a candidate will clear every interview. The goal is demonstrated competence, clear reasoning, and honest descriptions of lab experience.

Recommended time frames below are instructional estimates, not employer guarantees. Advancement depends on the practical assessment, not attendance.

| Starting point | Recommended instruction | Reasonable outcome |
|---|---|---|
| Already manages Linux services, users, networking, and filesystems | 7 days at 7 focused hours daily | Interview revision and evidence of integrated troubleshooting |
| Knows shell basics but needs substantial prompting | 4 weeks at 30 focused hours weekly | Foundation for supervised junior Linux or infrastructure duties |
| Beginner in both Linux and networking | 8–12 weeks at 20–25 focused hours weekly | Foundational competence followed by the four week lab sequence |
| Seeking independent responsibility for all listed requirements | Continued supervised operations beyond the course | Experience with production incidents, real hardware changes, and organizational procedures |

The four week path is the recommended default. The seven day path uses the same lab, with fewer repetitions. An experienced candidate may complete it sooner; a beginner should repeat modules until they meet the gates.

### Entry assessment

Give each student a clean Linux VM and 60 minutes. Ask them to create a non-root account, grant sudo, configure a persistent IP, install and enable a service, identify its port, create a persistent mount on an assigned blank disk, and diagnose a deliberately stopped service. They must explain their commands and preserve access.

Students may use installed man pages. Score each of the seven tasks out of 10: correct result 4, explanation 2, persistent verification 2, safe execution 2. Require at least 56/70 with no destructive mistake to enter the one week sprint. Students who need command-by-command prompting should use the longer path.

