Backup or recovery reference
Communication and handoff
Actual result and completion time
```

## 17 Final mock test

Use a fresh assigned VM and a different unique address or application label from the rehearsed exercise. Permit official documentation and man pages, but no trainer prompting or another student's configuration. Keep the instructor answer key separate. Grade individual work even when instruction was performed in pairs.

### Candidate brief

You are supporting an internal development service. Deploy it with accountable administration, persistent storage and restricted network access. Resolve assigned incidents, restore data, and hand off the service. Explain your work to the development lead. The R620 and shared management services must remain available.

### Four hour practical

| Time | Task | Points |
|---|---|---:|
| 0–35 min | Inventory assigned resources, validate guest IP and shared VLAN path | 15 |
| 35–80 min | Named admin, persistent web service and reboot proof | 15 |
| 80–120 min | Assigned blank disk, LVM, persistent mount and data proof | 15 |
| 120–150 min | Authorized and denied access, SELinux and SSH evidence | 15 |
| 150–185 min | Diagnose two assigned faults with evidence | 20 |
| 185–210 min | Restore designated file and explain VM restore evidence from Lab 5 | 10 |
| 210–240 min | Incident report, change handoff, hardware or lifecycle scenario | 10 |
| | Total | 100 |

XO full-VM restore can be slow and resource-dependent; it is mandatory in Lab 5 but not forced into the 25-minute exam recovery slot. If the assessor has sufficient time and capacity, extend the assessment and run another VM restore.

**Pass standard:** at least 80/100, at least half of the points in each domain, and all mandatory gates below. A high total does not override a critical gap.

- Correct target disk confirmed before formatting.
- Management access preserved and no unauthorized shared-host change.
- Required service and filesystem survive a reboot.
- SELinux remains enforcing and unauthorized access test fails as intended.
- Each incident has evidence supporting the diagnosis and a verified recovery.
- Restore proof is present; describing a backup without testing recovery is insufficient.
- Student explains their own work without inventing production experience.

Stop the assessment for formatting an unassigned disk, exposing credentials, disabling safeguards as the unexplained final fix, or altering live shared infrastructure outside the agreed boundary. Restore the environment and assign remediation before reassessment.

### Scoring method

For each domain, award approximately half its points for working behavior, a quarter for diagnosis and explanation, and a quarter for persistent or recovery evidence and documentation. Partial credit applies to a sound diagnosis even if the student runs out of time, but mandatory gates still apply. Keep assessor notes on prompting, unsafe actions and unverified claims.

## 18 Interview practice with expected answer elements

Conduct a 45–60 minute interview after the practical. Use follow-up questions tied to the candidate's actual evidence. The answer elements below guide the assessor; they are not scripts to memorize.

| Question | Expected answer elements |
|---|---|
| SSH fails but ping works. What do you inspect? | IP reachability differs from TCP service; route, port, listener, sshd logs, firewall, credentials and key policy |
| A site works locally but not remotely. What next? | Listening address, source and path, TCP test or capture, active firewall zone, route and VLAN evidence |
| Why can two guests communicate without the router? | Same-subnet traffic uses L2 switching; same-host traffic may remain virtual |
| How does VLAN 20 reach VLAN 30? | Guest gateway ARP, trunk tag, router subinterface and route, destination VLAN and ARP |
| Ping by IP works but name access fails. Why? | hosts or DNS lookup; getent follows configured resolution; wrong answer versus unreachable resolver |
| Why is a mount missing after reboot? | fstab UUID, syntax, filesystem, dependencies, NetworkManager or network readiness for remote storage |
| Why did growing an LV not increase df output? | Filesystem still needs growth; confirm filesystem type; XFS grows but cannot shrink |
| df shows full but du looks small. How can that happen? | Deleted open files, mount obscuring data, permissions, reserved space and differing scope; inspect before deletion |
| Is low free RAM a problem? | Available memory, cache, swap activity, pressure and workload symptoms; compare interval samples |
| What does high load average prove? | Demand from runnable or uninterruptible tasks; inspect CPU, I/O and process states |
| A VM is slow. Is the physical disk failing? | Guest metrics alone insufficient; correlate XO, host storage, controller events and concurrent workloads |
| An HTTP file is readable but Apache returns denied access. Why? | Directory traversal permissions, configuration, SELinux context and AVCs; repair persistently |
| How would you replace a failed RAID member? | Verify controller state and exact bay, backups, compatible replacement, change plan, rebuild monitoring; no blind pull |
| How do you approach a DIMM error? | Event and slot evidence, population rules, compatible part, outage, ESD, diagnostics and post-change verification |
| Is firmware rollback always possible? | Vendor-specific limitations; compatibility, release notes, recovery path and forward-fix option |
| Snapshot, RAID and backup: what differs? | Reset point, availability from redundancy, independent recoverable copy; failure domains matter |
| What are RPO and RTO? | Data-loss and recovery-time targets; measured restore reconciles them with real capability |
| Can this one-host lab demonstrate HA? | No host failover; describe additional host, network, storage and supported design requirements |
| How do you plan EOL mitigation? | Exact lifecycle notice, support and patch risk, inventory, replacement owner, migration and rollback tests |
| An urgent feature conflicts with an outage. What do you do? | Communicate impact, propose restoration priority, obtain scope decision, document owner and follow-up |
| What did you do when the developer's service failed? | Actual lab symptom, journal evidence, environment mismatch, reproduction, fix and test |

Score six categories from 0 to 4: Linux, networking, storage, security, diagnosis, and communication. A 0 is no useful answer; 1 is memorized terms; 2 is partial reasoning; 3 is an accurate sequence; 4 is accurate reasoning tied to observed evidence and tradeoffs. Require at least 18/24 and no category below 2. A candidate who passes the lab but cannot explain it needs additional practice.

## 19 Remediation and readiness reporting

Do not issue a broad job-ready label for attendance. Report competence by domain and the conditions under which it was demonstrated.

| Assessment result | Next action |
|---|---|
| Practical and interview pass; all gates met | Apply for roles matching demonstrated scope; continue weekly unseen incidents |
| 65–79 practical or one weak interview domain | Repeat failed modules for 3–5 focused sessions; reassess with different fault |
| Below 65 or frequent command prompting | Return to foundations and use the four week path |
| Safety gate failed | Review boundaries, supervised repetition and a fresh assessment |
| Hardware, AP or appliance work only simulated | Record the gap and arrange supervised physical practice |

Give each student a readiness record listing scores, independent tasks, prompted tasks, simulated tasks and outstanding gaps. Use “demonstrated in a training lab” where appropriate. For a role demanding strong working experience, lab competence should support an application, not be represented as years of production work.

### Portfolio checklist

- Inventory and labeled port map.
- Topology and unique IP allocation.
- Sanitized router and switch configuration with verification.
- Persistent Linux user, service and storage evidence.
- Firewall allow and deny tests and SELinux diagnosis.
- Baseline and bounded-load performance interpretation.
- Three incident reports covering network, storage and service or security faults.
- File and VM restore evidence with measured time and backup limitations.
- Development environment handoff and repeatable deployment evidence.
- Hardware procedure record distinguishing performed and simulated work.
- Lifecycle register, change request and blocker-resolution record.

Example honest interview statement: “In an NIT Academy lab, I built VLAN-backed Linux VMs on XCP-ng, configured Cisco inter-VLAN routing, managed LVM and NFS, restricted service access, diagnosed injected incidents and tested recovery. My evidence includes the configurations, logs and restore results. My hands-on hardware work covered the components listed in my assessment; other procedures were simulated.”

## 20 Extensions needed for broader job coverage

Start with your existing equipment. Add resources only when a defined training gap requires them.

| Gap | Extension exercise | Evidence required |
|---|---|---|
| Stateful hardware firewall | Borrow or use an authorized appliance; zones, policy, NAT, logs, backup and restore | Sessions, allowed and denied traffic, recoverable configuration |
| Wireless AP operation | Use an isolated AP; SSID to VLAN, authentication, channel and client diagnosis | Client connection, VLAN mapping and troubleshooting record |
| Multiple routers | Add an approved virtual or physical router and routed link; static routing then OSPF | Adjacency, learned prefixes and link-failure diagnosis |
| Multi-switch operation | Add compatible hardware; trunks, STP, LACP, stacking only if supported | Failover behavior, port roles and cable map |
| Host resilience | Add a second supported host and reviewed storage/network design | Actual workload recovery or migration and measured outage |
| Independent backup failure domain | External destination with sufficient retention and access control | Restore after loss of the original VM or simulated source unavailability |
| NVMe and broader component replacement | Spare compatible hardware and supervised parts | Component-specific before and after diagnostics |
| Central monitoring and logs | Dedicated monitoring and log server | Alert, incident acknowledgement, recovery and noise tuning |
| Fleet automation | Version-controlled Ansible or scripts on several guests | Repeatable deployment, drift detection and rollback |

An emulator can provide routing practice if lawful images and access are available. Mark simulated outcomes accurately. These additions extend training; they should not all be squeezed into the first week.

